Infra Level Common Terraform Errors 
      for DevOps interview preparation 🚀 

If you’ve ever created infrastructure using Terraform, you know how one small misconfiguration can bring everything to a halt.

Here are some common Terraform errors that almost every DevOps engineer runs into 👇

1. Provider Installation/Version Error → Missing or incompatible provider
    plugin version.
2. Backend Configuration Error → Misconfigured S3/GCS backend or missing
    access permissions.
3. State Lock Error → Terraform state locked due to an interrupted or 
    parallel operation.
4. Resource Already Exists → Resource manually created outside Terraform 
    or duplicate definition.
5. Variable Definition Error → Missing required variables or wrong data 
    types in terraform.tfvars.
6. Dependency Cycle Error → Circular references between dependent 
     resources.
7. IAM Permission Denied → Insufficient permissions for resource creation 
    or modification.
8. Plan Refresh Failure → Inability to read or refresh remote state or data 
    sources.
9. Module Source Error → Missing or incorrect module path or version.
10. Destroy Failure → Dependent resources not deleted or modified outside
      Terraform.
11. Version Constraint Error → Conflicting provider or Terraform version 
       constraints.
12. Timeout Error → API response delay or throttling from the cloud 
       provider.
13. Output Reference Error → Invalid or undefined output variable 
       referenced in another module.
14.  Insufficient Quota Error → Hitting resource limits
15.  Invalid Resource Configuration → Unsupported arguments or missing 
        required fields.
16.  Remote State Access Error → Network or authentication issues 
        accessing remote state.
17.  Duplicate Resource Definition → Multiple resources declared with the 
        same name.
18.  Data Source Read Error → Terraform unable to fetch data from cloud 
        resources.
19.  Unsupported Argument Error → Using parameters not supported by a 
        specific provider version.
20.  Terraform Crash / Panic Error → Unexpected internal failure due to 
        corrupted state or large module structures.
21.  Invalid Function Call Error → Using wrong or unsupported interpolation
        functions in expressions.
22.  Resource Targeting Error → Incorrect use of -target flag leading to 
        partial or inconsistent deployments.
23. Missing Provider Credentials → Environment variables or credentials file 
       not configured properly.
24. Drift Detection Failure → Terraform state doesn’t match the actual 
       cloud resources.
25. Invalid Key/Value in Map or List → Misformatted variables in maps, lists, 
      or objects.
26. Local Exec/Remote Exec Failure → Provisioner scripts failing due to 
      command or permission issues.
